>act 5

The Dancing Gnu
A pub that has been trying to look historic since about last Tuesday. Low beams,
dark wood, a carpet whose pattern has given up. The door is south onto the High
Road; a snug lies west, a hatch in the floor leads down to the cellar, and a
back door opens east onto a yard. At a small corner table, a man in a damp
raincoat drinks alone, with the look of someone who is waiting for something to
happen, again.

Random lounges against the wall with her arms folded, looking at everything the
way a cat looks at a bath. 

Tricia McMillan is here, watching everyone with a journalist's patient eye. 

Ford is here, restless, taking in the place as though pricing it. 

[The score has just gone up by three hundred points.]

>ask barman for a sandwich
"Ploughman's," says the barman, and produces a plain pub sandwich of ham and
pickle from under a cloth. It is not a sandwich that Lamuella would recognise,
but it is honest. "On the house," he says, "given the day we're having."

"You brought the fish," says Ford quietly, with approval. "Good man."

Tricia gets a round in. Random sits with her bag in her lap, looking at
everything and trusting nothing. Something in her coat pocket makes a faint,
contented purring.

[The score has just gone up by ten points.]

>e

The Pub Yard
A cobbled yard behind the pub with bins, a stack of empty crates and a gate that
opens east onto the park. Beyond the gate you can see the lawn and the bulk tin
on it. The back door of the pub is west.

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

Tricia follows, taking in the place with a journalist's eye.

Random drifts in after you at a distance she clearly considers dignified.

>e

The Lawn
The middle of the park, just inside the tape, with the bulk tin looming over you
like a landed apartment block. A door in its side stands open, and on the grass
in front of it two creatures sit at a folding table with a very large chart,
looking worried. The pub yard is west.

Two Grebulons are bent over a chart on a folding table, arguing in the voices of
people who have been doing it for a century. 

Ford ambles in after you, towel over one shoulder.

Tricia follows, taking in the place with a journalist's eye.

Random drifts in after you at a distance she clearly considers dignified.

>give sandwich to grebulons
The nearer Grebulon takes it, eyes it, passes it to the other, who takes a bite
and looks up with great emotion. "This," says the first, "is hospitality. We had
forgotten. We had a button, you know, and no idea what it was for."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>ask grebulons about the button
"The button," says the nearer Grebulon, vaguely. "It is red. It is very large.
We were told to press it when the room was full. We don't remember what room. Or
what happens." A silence. "Everything, probably."

[The score has just gone up by five points.]

>ask grebulons about the chart
The chart is spread over the table, a huge, creased star-map covered in
annotations. One star, drawn in red, has been circled, scribbled out and re-
circled; beside it someone has written, in a loping hand: BEEBLEBROX'S FOLLY. DO
NOT PUBLISH.

